  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 4,
    "lesson_title": "Low-Pass Magnitude Response",
    "lesson_core_move": "Compute the RC low-pass magnitude response from its frequency response and interpret how it passes low frequencies while attenuating high frequencies.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Low-Pass Magnitude Response",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Low-Pass Magnitude Response",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The magnitude response is the gain curve $|H(j\\omega)|$ of the RC low-pass filter. Read it when you want to know how much a sinusoid at frequency $\\omega$ is scaled, without tracking the phase.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(j\\omega)=\\frac{1}{1+j\\omega RC}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from the known low-pass frequency response $H(j\\omega)=\\frac{1}{1+j\\omega RC}$. The magnitude response is the absolute value of that complex response, so the phase factor does not change the gain.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|H(j\\omega)|=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+(\\omega RC)^2}}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cleanest check is the zero-frequency case: at $\\omega=0$, the gain is $1$. So very low frequencies pass through the filter with essentially no attenuation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|H(j0)|=1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "At the opposite extreme, the gain falls toward $0$ as $\\omega$ gets large. That is the low-pass signature: low frequencies are passed, high frequencies are attenuated.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\lim_{\\omega\\to\\infty}|H(j\\omega)|=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the magnitude response condenses the filter into one nonnegative curve: near $1$ at low frequency and near $0$ at high frequency. That compact gain curve is the frequency-domain statement of pass-low, attenuate-high behavior.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Low-Pass Magnitude Response",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Computing |$H(j\\omega)$| from the Low-Pass Frequency Response",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $H(j\\omega)=\\frac{1}{1+j\\omega RC}$, compute $|H(j\\omega)|$ and simplify it to a real-valued gain expression.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the known RC low-pass frequency response $H(j\\omega)=\\frac{1}{1+j\\omega RC}$. The action is to take its magnitude and simplify the complex denominator until only a real gain remains.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\left|H(j\\omega)\\right|=\\left|\\frac{1}{1+j\\omega RC}\\right|=\\frac{|1|}{|1+j\\omega RC|}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The numerator has magnitude $1$. For the denominator, use the magnitude of $a+jb$ with $a=1$ and $b=\\omega RC$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|1+j\\omega RC|=\\sqrt{1+(\\omega RC)^2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute that denominator magnitude back into the gain expression.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|H(j\\omega)|=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+(\\omega RC)^2}}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That is the simplified RC low-pass magnitude response: a real, nonnegative gain formula written directly from the frequency response.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Low-Pass Magnitude Response",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Evaluating Low-Frequency Gain for the RC Low-Pass Filter",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Starting from $|H(j\\omega)|=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+(\\omega RC)^2}}$, compute the low-frequency gain $|H(j0)|$ and explain why the filter behaves like a pass-through near DC.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the low-frequency endpoint of the magnitude formula from the previous step. Use direct substitution at $\\omega=0$ in $|H(j\\omega)|=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+(\\omega RC)^2}}$ to evaluate the gain near DC.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|H(j\\omega)|=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+(\\omega RC)^2}}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute $\\omega=0$ and simplify the denominator. The squared term vanishes, so the response becomes a unit gain.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|H(j0)|=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+(0\\cdot RC)^2}}=1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the RC low-pass filter passes a constant or slowly varying component with gain $1$ at DC. That is the low-frequency endpoint that shows the filter behaves like a pass-through near zero frequency.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Low-Pass Magnitude Response",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Evaluating High-Frequency Gain for the RC Low-Pass Filter",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Using $|H(j\\omega)|=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+(\\omega RC)^2}}$, evaluate the high-frequency gain as $\\omega\\to\\infty$ and explain why the response goes to 0.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the high-frequency endpoint of the same magnitude formula. Use the limit $\\omega\\to\\infty$ on $|H(j\\omega)|=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+(\\omega RC)^2}}$ so we can see what happens to rapid oscillations.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|H(j\\omega)|=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+(\\omega RC)^2}}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "As $\\omega$ grows, the denominator grows without bound while the numerator stays $1$. That forces the gain to shrink toward zero.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\lim_{\\omega\\to\\infty}|H(j\\omega)|=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the RC low-pass filter attenuates high-frequency components: rapid oscillations are suppressed because their gain approaches zero.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Low-Pass Magnitude Response",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Interpreting the Low-Pass Magnitude Response as Passing and Attenuating Frequencies",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $|H(j\\omega)|=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+(\\omega RC)^2}}$, explain how the response behaves at low and high frequencies and use that to classify the filter as low-pass.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the same magnitude curve from the previous steps. We already know the low-frequency gain is $1$ at $\\omega=0$ and the high-frequency gain tends to $0$ as $\\omega\\to\\infty$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|H(j\\omega)|=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+(\\omega RC)^2}}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That means the curve starts near $1$ at low frequency and falls toward $0$ as frequency increases. So the response gives strong gain to slowly varying components and weak gain to rapid oscillations.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|H(j0)|=1,\\qquad \\lim_{\\omega\\to\\infty}|H(j\\omega)|=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Therefore the RC filter is low-pass: low frequencies are passed, while high frequencies are attenuated. The magnitude response is the frequency-domain summary of that behavior.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
